2.3 Fixed Wing Aircraft
We can distinguish between cyclical phenomena—associated with helicopter and wind turbine
blades and characterized by alternating dynamic stall and attachment—and transient
phenomena produced by purely pitch-up or pitch-down motion. In transient dynamic stall, the
external flowfield changes dynamically from one nominally steady state to another, often
characterized by delay-and-decay-type behavior [65]. One example is the attainment of so
called supermaneuverable aircraft that exploit the transient effects of the lift overshoot
associated with dynamic stall [66]. Here the objective is to exploit and harness dynamic stall
effects, to facilitate controlled flight and stability at excessively high angles-of-attack.
