3.6 Reducing the Parameter Space
In general, aerodynamic bodies translate and rotate with six degrees-of-freedom and therefore
can stall dynamically due to translation, rotation, or some combination thereof. When
evaluating a dynamic stall control method or strategy—either experimentally or
computationally—we would like it to have as general an application as possible, while
minimizing the test matrix. For pure pitching this can be done by changing the pitching
frequency alone—and not the angle-of-attack excursions—based on the principle of the
matched pitch-rate. In addition, we can extend the concept of pitch-plunge equivalence to justify
the adoption of pure pitching as being representative of plunging motion. These two concepts
are described below.
